      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i was reading about hiit workouts and kept seeing stuff about lactic acid. can u explain how it actually gets made in the muscles and how the body gets rid of it after a set? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lactic acid, a byproduct of intense exercise, particularly HIIT workouts, is a common concern for many. It's a natural process, but one that can be quite...gobsmacking, if you will. You see, when you engage in high-intensity interval training, your muscles rely heavily on anaerobic respiration, a process that doesn't require oxygen. This leads to the breakdown of glucose into lactic acid, a byproduct that's stored in your muscles.\n\nThink of it like a group of mischievous little goblins, rummaging through your muscle cells, looking for energy sources. They're like tiny, pesky creatures, devouring glucose and producing lactic acid as a byproduct. The more intense the exercise, the more goblins you'll have, and the more lactic acid they'll produce.\n\nNow, when you're done with a set, your body starts to clear out the lactic acid. It's a bit like a goblin cleanup crew, working tirelessly to remove the unwanted byproducts. The lactic acid is transported to the liver, where it's either converted back into glucose or excreted through the kidneys. It's a bit like a goblin's game of",
